13:27:29 INFO catmaster.llm.factory | [llm.http.raw_post] provider=openrouter model=openai/gpt-5.4:online method=POST url=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions body={"messages":[{"content":"Your goal is to select the most relevant tools for answering the user's query.\nIMPORTANT: The following core tools are already available and do not need to be selected: read_text_file, read_multiple_files, search_files, list_directory, directory_tree, create_directory, write_file, edit_file, move_file.\nIMPORTANT: List the tool names in order of relevance, with the most relevant first. If you exceed the maximum number of tools, only the first 20 will be used.","role":"system"},{"content":"<context_pack>\nTask goal:\nPrepare and relax the common MACE reference states: gas-phase CO and the clean constrained Fe(110) slab.\n\nTask detail:\nUse skills `adsorbate-and-intermediate-generation` and `mace-screening-and-relaxation`. Start from `slabs/fe110_raw/mp-13_h110_t0_2x2_fix2layers.vasp` unchanged as the clean slab input. Create CO from SMILES (`[C]#[O]` or equivalent neutral CO input accepted by the tool) into a dedicated molecule/reference directory. Run MACE geometry relaxations for (1) isolated CO and (2) the clean constrained slab. Keep MACE `output_root` separate from each input directory. Preserve slab selective dynamics/constraints; do not alter lattice for the slab unless the tool default would do so, in which case explicitly disable lattice relaxation if available. After relaxations, collect the relaxed endpoint structure paths, batch-state paths, and the final energies needed later for adsorption-energy evaluation. If endpoint energies are not clearly/consistently available from relax outputs, run a normalized MACE single-point stage on the relaxed CO and relaxed clean slab only, and report those SP energies as the reference energies. Do not prepare or run any VASP or NEB jobs.\n\nExpected outputs:\n- Project-relative path to the generated CO structure file.\n- Project-relative MACE output root for relaxed CO.\n- Project-relative MACE output root for relaxed clean slab.\n- Project-relative relaxed endpoint structure path for CO.\n- Project-relative relaxed endpoint structure path for clean slab.\n- Reference-energy values (with units, eV) for CO and clean slab, plus where they were read from.\n- Any batch-state/summary files needed as evidence for later adsorption-energy calculations.\n\nSuggested tools:\ncreate_molecule_from_smiles, mace_relax_batch, mace_sp_batch, read_text_file, list_directory\n\nReference hint:\n- Input slab: `slabs/fe110_raw/mp-13_h110_t0_2x2_fix2layers.vasp`\n- Keep input/output trees separate for MACE runs\n- No VASP or NEB steps are allowed\n- Use common reference energies from one consistent MACE stage if possible\n\nWorkspace policy:\nProject files policy:\n- Treat \".\" as the project files root.\n- Reference absolute files root (orientation only): /home/chenhh/python_projects/CatMaster/project_space/skill_6/files\n- Use relative paths for filesystem function-tool arguments; keep returned paths relative to \".\".\n- Absolute paths are fallback-only references; if used for filesystem tools, they must be under project files root.\n- Never use metadata paths in filesystem function-tool arguments.\n- Use search_files / list_directory / directory_tree to discover files.\n- Use read_text_file with head/tail for progressive disclosure.\n- Use bash_exec for shell commands, content grep, parser invocation, and external binaries.\n- Role: task_runner\n\nWorkspace absolute root (reference only, do not pass directly to filesystem tools):\n/home/chenhh/python_projects/CatMaster/project_space/skill_6/files\n\nMemory index excerpt:\n# MEMORY (AUTOLOADED INDEX)\n\n## What this is\n- Autoloaded memory index for this project.\n- Keep this file concise and pointer-first.\n- Put details in MEMORY/topics/*.md.\n\n## Latest State\n- Current focus: Study CO adsorption on a bcc Fe(110) surface using a MACE-only fast screening workflow. Build the slab, generate likely adsorption candidates, relax them with MACE, and report a preliminary adsorption-energy ranking with the key output paths. Do not prepare or run any VASP or NEB calculations.\n- Current milestone: (empty)\n- Latest run artifact: (empty)\n- Next action: (empty)\n\n## Top Constraints\n1. (empty)\n\n## Active Open Questions\n1. (empty)\n\n## Pointers\n- Facts / decisions: MEMORY/topics/FACTS.md\n- Files / artifacts: MEMORY/topics/FILES.md\n- Constraints: MEMORY/topics/CONSTRAINTS.md\n- Open questions: MEMORY/topics/QUESTIONS.md\n- Runbook: MEMORY/topics/RUNBOOK.md\n\n## Keyword Map\n- GOAL: objective, success criteria, scope, non-goals\n- FACTS: definition, verified, result, decision\n- FILES: path, artifact, dataset, output, script\n- CONSTRAINTS: must, cannot, policy, budget\n- QUESTIONS: unknown, blocked, clarify, decide\n- RUNBOOK: steps, checklist, verify\n\n</context_pack>\n","role":"user"}],"model":"openai/gpt-5.4:online","reasoning_effort":"minimal","response_format":{"type":"json_schema","json_schema":{"name":"ToolSelectionResponse","description":"Use to select relevant tools.","strict":false,"schema":{"properties":{"tools":{"description":"Tools to use. Place the most relevant tools first.","items":{"anyOf":[{"const":"create_molecule_from_smiles","description":"Build a 3D molecule from a SMILES string using RDKit. Specially, pay attention to the formal charge of the SMILES string, RDkit will check the charge and add Hs to the molecule to make it neutral.","type":"string"},{"const":"mace_relax_batch","description":"Submit multiple MACE relaxations in one DPDispatcher submission. Preferred tool for batch MACE relaxations.","type":"string"},{"const":"mace_sp_batch","description":"Run MACE single-point jobs for structures under one directory.","type":"string"},{"const":"vasp_relax_prepare","description":"Prepare MPRelaxSet-based VASP relax inputs with calc_type presets and k_product k-mesh.\n    Also can be modified for other calculation types by overriding INCAR via the user_incar_settings.\n    Support exts: .vasp, .cif\n\n    Baseline DFT settings follow Materials Project MPRelaxSet (key defaults):\n    - PBE54 POTCARS, ENCUT=520 eV, PREC=Accurate, ISPIN=2, IBRION=2, LREAL=AUTO, LASPH=True, LWAVE=False, LCHARG=False.\n    - Additional overrides applied based on calc_type: ISMEAR=0, SIGMA=0.1 for bulk/slab; ISMEAR=0, SIGMA=0.01 for gas.\n    - ISIF policy: bulk with relax_cell=False -> ISIF=2; bulk with relax_cell=True -> ISIF=3.\n      slab and gas always use ISIF=2, where slab forces KPOINTS z=1 and gas forces KPOINTS 1x1x1 with ISYM=0, LREAL=False.\n    - Improved convergence settings: EDIFF=1e-6; NSW=500; EDIFFG=-0.02;\n    - LORBIT is controlled by compute_dos (default False -> LORBIT=0, True -> LORBIT=11).\n    - KPOINTS is generated by k*a=k_product, where a is the lattice constant in Angstrom. Set to 30 by default and sufficient for most systems.\n    - DFT-D3 and DFT+U are disabled by default. Enable them if the system is adsorption system(DFT-D3) or transition metal oxide system(DFT+U).","type":"string"},{"const":"vasp_sp_prepare","description":"Prepare MPRelaxSet-based VASP single-point inputs for structures under a file or directory. Parameters are the same as VaspRelaxPrepareInput, but with single_point relaxation enabled (NSW=0, IBRION=-1).","type":"string"},{"const":"build_slab","description":"Build slabs for all terminations of a Miller index from bulk structure(s).\n\n    Provide exactly one of bulk_structure or bulk_dir. When bulk_dir is used, output_root is required.\n    Batch outputs are written under output_root/<slab_id>/..., where slab_id encodes the relative input path\n    (without suffix) using '__'.","type":"string"},{"const":"fix_atoms_by_layers","description":"Fix (freeze) the bottom N atomic layers of slab structure(s).\n\n    Provide exactly one of structure_ref or structure_dir. When structure_dir is used, output_dir is required.\n    Batch outputs are written to output_dir/<slab_id>.vasp and a summary JSON is written to\n    output_dir/batch_fix_atoms_by_layers.json.","type":"string"},{"const":"fix_atoms_by_height","description":"Fix (freeze) atoms within specified z ranges of slab structure(s).\n\n    Provide exactly one of structure_ref or structure_dir. When structure_dir is used, output_dir is required.\n    Batch outputs are written to output_dir/<slab_id>.vasp and a summary JSON is written to\n    output_dir/batch_fix_atoms_by_height.json.","type":"string"},{"const":"supercell","description":"Create supercells from structure file(s). THIS WILL LOSE ALL SELECTIVE DYNAMICS INFORMATION.\n    Provide exactly one of structure_file or structure_dir. When structure_dir is used, output_dir is required.\n    Batch outputs are written to output_dir/<structure_id>.vasp and a summary JSON is written to\n    output_dir/batch_supercell.json.\n\n    The JSON format is:\n    {\n      \"results\": [\n        {\"input_rel\": \"a/CO.vasp\", \"structure_id\": \"a__CO\", \"output_rel\": \"out/a__CO.vasp\", \"natoms\": 2}\n      ]\n    }","type":"string"},{"const":"enumerate_adsorption_sites","description":"Enumerate adsorption sites on a slab using ASF. Use it only for small scale placement.\n\n    The tool writes a JSON list to output_json:\n    [\n      {\"label\": \"ontop_0\", \"kind\": \"ontop\", \"cart_coords\": [x, y, z]}\n    ]\n    The returned tool data includes the workspace-relative path in \"sites\" and \"sites_json_rel\".","type":"string"},{"const":"place_adsorbate","description":"Place an adsorbate molecule on a slab. Use it only if for small scale placement.\n    This tool keeps selective dynamics of the original slab structure and allows adsorbate to move freely.\n    It writes metadata sidecar <output>.meta.json and an ads_indices index JSON.","type":"string"},{"const":"generate_batch_adsorption_structures","description":"Generate multiple adsorbed structures up to max_structures. Suitable for large scale placement.\n    This tool keeps selective dynamics of the original slab structure and allows adsorbate to move freely.\n    Input can be a single slab file (slab_file) or a directory of slab files (slab_dir).\n    When slab_dir is used, each slab gets its own subdirectory under output_dir. Max_structures applies per slab.\n    For nested slab_dir layouts, slab_id encodes the relative path (without suffix) using '__'.\n\n    The tool writes a JSON list to output_dir/batch_structures.json and a per-structure\n    metadata sidecar <output>.meta.json with ads_indices.\n    It also writes output_dir/ads_indices.json as an index file:\n    [\n      {\n        \"slab_file_rel\": \"slabs/fe111.vasp\",\n        \"slab_id\": \"fe111\",\n        \"label\": \"ontop_0\",\n        \"output_poscar_rel\": \"adsorption/batch/fe111/ontop_0.vasp\",\n        \"ads_indices_added\": [36],\n        \"ads_indices\": [36]\n      }\n    ]\n    The returned tool data replaces \"structures\" with the workspace-relative path to this JSON file.","type":"string"},{"const":"make_neb_geometry","description":"Generate NEB interpolation geometries in nebmake.pl style.\n\n    The tool validates that initial and final structures have the same element order, atom count,\n    and lattice (within tolerance). Images are written under output_dir/00..(NI+1) with output_filename.\n    Selective dynamics constraints are preserved if present in the input files (ASE handles constraints).","type":"string"},{"const":"make_neb_incar","description":"Generate a VTST NEB/CI-NEB INCAR based on a template INCAR.\n\n    The tool parses the template INCAR (ignores comments/blank lines, case-insensitive keys),\n    applies NEB overrides, and writes the final INCAR along with a patch JSON.","type":"string"},{"const":"vasp_execute_batch","description":"Submit VASP runs in one DPDispatcher submission (single-folder or recursive batch modes).","type":"string"},{"const":"mp_search_materials","description":"Search Materials Project with flexible criteria and write results to CSV.\n\n    Common criteria keys (pass via `criteria`):\n      - material_ids (list[str] or str)\n      - elements (list[str] or str)\n      - chemsys (str)\n      - formula (str)\n      - is_stable (bool)\n      - energy_above_hull ([min, max])\n      - formation_energy ([min, max])\n      - band_gap ([min, max])\n      - crystal_system (str)\n      - spacegroup_number / spacegroup_symbol\n      - num_sites ([min, max])\n      - density ([min, max])\n      - volume ([min, max])\n      - num_elements ([min, max])\n    Common fields for output rows (pass via `fields`):\n      material_id, formula_pretty (or formula), energy_above_hull, formation_energy_per_atom (or formation_energy),\n      band_gap, crystal_system, spacegroup_number, spacegroup_symbol, nsites (or num_sites), density, volume.","type":"string"},{"const":"mp_download_structure","description":"Download one or more structures from Materials Project into the workspace.","type":"string"},{"const":"bash_exec","description":"Execute a multi-line bash script inside the workspace (default) and return stdout/stderr.\n    Network access is disabled by default using Linux network namespaces (unshare).\n    Symbolic link operations are disabled; use copy/move operations instead.\n    Stdout/stderr are returned as-is and projection/offload is handled centrally.\n    Keep output short in scripts and print one-line summaries when possible.","type":"string"},{"const":"apply_aider_edits","description":"Apply Aider SEARCH/REPLACE edit blocks to workspace files.","type":"string"}]},"title":"Tools","type":"array"}},"required":["tools"],"type":"object"}}},"stream":false,"temperature":1.0,"prompt_cache_retention":"24h"}
